er"}, "order": 13}, {"outputs": [{"name": "STRING", "links": [72], "label": "STRING", "type": "STRING", "localized_name": "STRING"}], "color": "#332922", "widgets_values": ["You turn a user's image idea into one detailed caption for a text-to-image model. The model learned from captions that describe finished images, so write the caption as a description of the image itself, not as instructions.\n\nKeep the request\n- Keep everything the user specified: subjects, counts, actions, relationships, medium, style, mood, colors and anything they excluded. Never contradict or drop it.\n- Fill in what they left open with concrete visual choices: setting, composition and framing, viewpoint, lighting, materials and textures, color palette, and the artistic medium.\n- Describe only what can be seen. Leave out sounds, smells, backstory and intentions.\n- Do not invent real-world facts the user did not give, such as specific dates, prices, names or specifications.\n\nText in the image\n- Quote every piece of visible text exactly, in double quotes. Keep the user's wording, language, spelling, capitalization, punctuation and line breaks; never translate it.\n- If the user asks for a designed image (poster, cover, menu, label, sign, infographic) without giving its text, write short, specific copy that fits instead of placeholders.\n- Describe where each text sits and how it looks: position, reading order, alignment, line breaks, relative size, font style, color and spacing.\n- Do not mention watermarks or artist signatures.\n\nWriting\n- Write in English, in plain prose: one to three paragraphs, usually 80 to 200 words, longer only when there is a lot of text to place.\n- Open with the medium and the main subject, then the setting and composition, then lighting, colors and finer details.\n- Give positions with words such as left, right, top, bottom, foreground and background. No coordinates, lists, headings, JSON or Markdown.\n- No quality keywords such as \"masterpiece\", \"best quality\", \"8k\" or \"highly detailed\", and no negative prompt.\n\nReply with the caption only: no title, introduction, explanation or surrounding quotes."], "inputs": [{"widget": {"name": "value"}, "name": "value", "label": "value", "type": "STRING", "localized_name": "value"}], "flags": {"collapsed": false}, "type": "PrimitiveStringMultiline", "title": "Text (System prompt)", "mode": 0, "bgcolor": "#593930", "size": [490, 550], "pos": [771.9920654296875, 1224.39599609375], "id": 26, "properties": {"cnr_id": "comfy-core", "ver": "0.41.0", "widget_ue_connectable": {}, "Node name for S&R": "PrimitiveStringMultiline"}, "order": 5}, {"outputs": [{"name": "STRING", "links": [89], "label": "STRING", "type": "STRING", "localized_name": "STRING"}], "color": "#332922", "widgets_values": ["Create a polished urban lifestyle editorial illustration with the sensibility of an upscale European/American lifestyle magazine from the 1990s. Use crisp, consistent-weight linework and architectural line art, with linear urban buildings and a multi-figure everyday city-life scene. Keep the figures stylized as illustration, not photorealistic. Use the configured fixed identity reference for the visible human figure(s) while preserving the drawn style.\n\nBuild the image from flat color blocks, minimal shadows, and a restrained, low-saturation retro palette. Give exactly one subject a high-saturation color treatment so it becomes the clear visual focal point; the subject may be a person or an object, and should remain the only vivid accent. Keep all other colors muted and harmonious.\n\nArrange the composition like a luxury lifestyle magazine cover, with refined editorial hierarchy, balanced negative space, and a fashionable, relaxed urban mood. The source provides no specific city, activity, focal-object identity, or cover copy; keep those details general and do not invent names or factual headlines. No arbitrary readable text, logos, or watermarks."], "inputs": [{"widget": {"name": "value"}, "name": "value", "label": "value", "type": "STRING", "localized_name": "value"}], "flags": {}, "type": "PrimitiveStringMultiline", "title": "Text (User Prompt)", "mode": 0, "bgcolor": "#593930", "size": [430, 270], "pos": [240, 1240], "id": 25, "properties": {"cnr_id": "comfy-core", "ver": "0.41.0", "widget_ue_connectable": {}, "Node name for S&R": "PrimitiveStringMultiline"}, "order": 6}, {"outputs": [{"name": "image_urls", "links": [], "label": "image_urls", "type": "STRING", "localized_name": "image_urls"}, {"name": "images", "label": "images", "type": "IMAGE", "localized_name": "images"}], "color": "#332922", "widgets_values": ["moss_image_t2i"], "inputs": [{"name": "images", "link": 84, "label": "images", "type": "IMAGE", "localized_name": "images"}, {"widget": {"name": "filename_prefix"}, "name": "filename_prefix", "label": "filename_prefix", "type": "STRING", "localized_name": "filename_prefix"}], "flags": {}, "type": "SaveImage", "mode": 0, "bgcolor": "#593930", "size": [560, 740], "pos": [1860.5140380859375, 1844.6541748046875], "id": 45, "properties": {"cnr_id": "comfy-core", "ver": "0.41.0", "widget_ue_connectable": {}, "Node name for S&R": "SaveImage"}, "order": 15}], "extra": {"VHS_KeepIntermediate": false, "links_added_by_ue": [], "VHS_MetadataImage": false, "ue_links": [], "pixaromaGroups": [], "0246.VERSION": [0, 0, 4], "VHS_latentpreviewrate": 0, "frontendVersion": "1.55.17", "VHS_latentpreview": true, "ds": {"offset": [-1672.3710202437835, -1990.1216032662253], "scale": 2.067737843860753}}, "groups": [{"color": "#3f789e", "font_size": 240, "flags": {}, "id": 12, "title": "B站，Youtube：T8star-Aix", "bounding": [-198.90896606445312, 617.3018188476562, 2836.38525390625, 427.2743835449219]}], "links": [[53, 30, 0, 29, 0, "CLIP"], [54, 33, 0, 29, 1, "STRING"], [56, 29, 0, 31, 1, "CONDITIONING"], [57, 36, 0, 31, 2, "CONDITIONING"], [61, 30, 0, 36, 0, "CLIP"], [62, 31, 0, 37, 0, "LATENT"], [63, 32, 0, 37, 1, "VAE"], [65, 35, 0, 31, 3, "LATENT"], [69, 